.# Abstract

This memo defines Taiclock, an HTTP-accessible service
that returns the current time as a TAI64N label,
optionally signed with Ed25519. The service is reached
at the well-known URI `/.well-known/taiclock` and is
intended for clients that need an authenticated
wall-clock reading without running an NTP stack or
trusting an unauthenticated TLS handshake clock. The
signing scheme uses a length-prefixed, domain-separated
framing and publishes verification keys as DNS TXT
records in a DKIM-inspired selector layout.

{mainmatter}

# Introduction

Many small clients need an accurate wall-clock reading
to validate certificates, evaluate signature freshness,
or stamp local events. Two common sources fall short:

- An NTP client is intrusive on constrained or sandboxed
  runtimes and exposes the device to a separate trust
  domain.
- The TLS handshake clock is not authenticated by any
  party the client can name; an attacker who can serve
  a valid certificate (for instance via a misissued
  intermediate or via a cooperating CA) can also adjust
  the apparent time.

Taiclock addresses these by serving the current time as
a 25-byte TAI64N [@TAI64] label over HTTP, with an
optional Ed25519 [@!RFC8032] signature bound to a
client-supplied nonce. Verification keys are published
in DNS as TXT records under a DKIM-style [@?RFC6376]
selector, so a verifier can rotate keys without
coordinating with clients.

The protocol is deliberately small. A compliant
implementation fits in a single fetch handler, has no
state beyond its signing key, and is independent of any
specific HTTP framework or runtime.

# Conventions and Definitions

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL",
"SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED",
"NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this
document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14
[@!RFC2119] [@!RFC8174] when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.

The terms "TAI" and "TAI64N" are used as defined in
[@TAI64].

The term "selector" is used in the DKIM
sense [@?RFC6376]: an opaque, host-scoped label that
selects one verification key from possibly many
published under the same host.

# Resource

A Taiclock service is reached at the well-known
URI [@?RFC8615]:

~~~
/.well-known/taiclock
~~~

A request URI MUST be the exact path above. Query
strings, if any, are ignored by this version of the
protocol and MUST NOT change the response.

## Methods

A server MUST accept `GET` and `HEAD`. A server MUST
respond `405 Method Not Allowed` to any other method,
and MUST include `Allow: GET, HEAD` on the 405
response.

A `HEAD` response MUST carry the same headers as the
corresponding `GET` response and MUST NOT include a
body.

## Successful Response

A successful `GET` response has status `200 OK` and a
body consisting of exactly 25 ASCII bytes: the TAI64N
external representation of the server's current time,
formatted per [@TAI64]:

~~~
'@' hex16(seconds-since-TAI-epoch) hex8(nanoseconds)
~~~

where `hex16` and `hex8` denote 16 and 8 lowercase
hexadecimal digits respectively. The leading `@` is
literal.

The following headers MUST be present:

| Header           | Value                                |
|------------------|--------------------------------------|
| `Content-Type`   | `application/tai64n`                 |
| `Content-Length` | `25`                                 |
| `Cache-Control`  | `no-store`                           |
| `TAI-Leap-Seconds` | decimal count, e.g. `37`           |

`TAI-Leap-Seconds` carries the offset, in seconds,
between TAI and UTC at the moment of the response. It
is informational; the TAI64N label is the canonical
time. A server MUST emit it on every successful
response.

A server MAY also offer the vendor type
`application/vnd.djb.tai64n` for clients that prefer
to negotiate it explicitly. A server SHOULD default
to `application/tai64n` when no preference is
expressed.

## Nonce Echo

A client MAY send a request header `TAI-Nonce` carrying
an opaque, application-defined byte string in the
header value. If present, the server MUST echo the
header verbatim on the response.

A server MUST NOT interpret the nonce. Clients SHOULD
choose nonces that are unpredictable to a network
observer when freshness matters; 16 random bytes
encoded as base64url are sufficient for typical use.

# Signing

A Taiclock service MAY be configured with an Ed25519
[@!RFC8032] signing key and a selector. When both are
configured, the server signs every response that
carries a `TAI-Nonce`.

A signed successful response carries two additional
headers:

| Header              | Value                                  |
|---------------------|----------------------------------------|
| `TAI-Key-Selector`  | the selector configured on the server  |
| `TAI-Signature`     | base64url, no padding, of the signature|

The selector MUST match the production
`[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,63}` so that it forms a single,
DNS-safe label.

## Framed Payload

The signed payload is the concatenation, in order, of
the following fields:

~~~
domain-tag    = "taiclock-v1" 0x00
label-bytes   = 25 octets ; the response body
leap-u32be    = 4 octets  ; TAI-Leap-Seconds, big-endian unsigned
selector-len  = 1 octet   ; length of selector-bytes, 1..63
selector-bytes = 1..63 octets ; the selector, US-ASCII
nonce-bytes   = octets    ; the request TAI-Nonce, verbatim

payload = domain-tag label-bytes leap-u32be
          selector-len selector-bytes nonce-bytes
~~~

The trailing NUL on `domain-tag` is significant: it
prevents extension of the tag into adjacent bytes by
an attacker who controls the protocol version string.

The selector is length-prefixed by a single byte. A
downgrade attacker who rewrites `TAI-Key-Selector` on
the wire cannot match an existing signature without
also matching its length, which forces a hash
collision under Ed25519.

The nonce occupies the tail of the payload. Its length
is therefore self-delimiting: a verifier that has
recovered `domain-tag`, `label-bytes`, `leap-u32be`,
`selector-len`, and `selector-bytes` reconstructs the
nonce as the remaining input.

## When to Sign

A server SHOULD sign a response if and only if all of
the following hold:

- The server is configured with a key and selector.
- The request carried a `TAI-Nonce` header.
- The response is a 200 to `GET` or `HEAD`.

Servers MUST NOT sign 405 responses.

A request without a nonce MAY receive an unsigned
response even when the server is configured to sign.
This allows trivially cacheable probes to coexist with
authenticated reads, though the response itself MUST
remain `Cache-Control: no-store` because the label is
time-sensitive.

# Key Publication

Verification keys are published as DNS TXT records.
The owner name is

~~~
<selector> "._taiclock." <host>
~~~

where `<host>` is the authority component of the URL at
which `/.well-known/taiclock` is served, and
`<selector>` is the value the server emits in
`TAI-Key-Selector`.

The TXT record value is a single string, of length at
most 255 octets, formatted as a tag-value list in the
DKIM/DMARC style:

~~~
v=tai1; k=ed25519; p=<base64-of-32-raw-pubkey-bytes>
~~~

| Tag | Value |
|-----|-------|
| `v` | Protocol version. `tai1` for the framing in this document. |
| `k` | Key algorithm. `ed25519` is the only algorithm currently defined. |
| `p` | Public key, standard base64 of the 32 raw Ed25519 public-key bytes. |

A verifier MUST treat unknown tags as informational.
A verifier MUST treat an unknown `v` or `k` as an
unrecoverable failure for that selector and MUST NOT
fall back silently.

## Key Rotation

To rotate a key, an operator publishes a new TXT record
under a new selector, then reconfigures the server to
sign with the new selector. The old TXT record SHOULD
remain in DNS until any cached responses signed under
the old selector have aged out of verifier caches; at
that point the old TXT record MAY be removed.

Verifiers cache by selector, not by host. A response
signed under selector `S` remains verifiable while the
TXT record at `S._taiclock.<host>` is reachable and
its parsed value is unchanged.

# Verification

A verifier reads the response and reconstructs the
framed payload as defined in (#signing). The verifier
MUST:

1. Confirm that the request `TAI-Nonce` matches the
   response `TAI-Nonce` byte-for-byte.
2. Confirm that the response carries
   `TAI-Key-Selector` and `TAI-Signature`.
3. Resolve the TXT record at
   `<selector>._taiclock.<host>` and parse the
   `v`, `k`, and `p` tags.
4. Verify the Ed25519 signature using the public key
   from `p` over the framed payload.

Steps 1, 2, and 4 MUST all succeed. A verifier MUST
NOT accept a label whose signature is absent or whose
signature does not verify.

A verifier that does not implement the optional
signature path MAY still consume the unsigned label,
provided it understands that it is trusting the
underlying transport with no protocol-level
authentication of the time value.

# Security Considerations

## Threat Model

Taiclock authenticates a single time reading at the
moment a verifier asks. It does not establish a long-
running synchronised clock and does not bound network
delay; a verifier is responsible for treating the
returned label as a measurement subject to the
round-trip time of its request.

## Replay

A response is bound to its request only via the
`TAI-Nonce` echo and its inclusion in the signed
payload. A verifier that reuses a nonce, or that omits
the nonce, gains no replay protection. Verifiers
SHOULD generate a fresh, unpredictable nonce for each
request.

## Domain Separation

The `taiclock-v1\0` prefix prevents an Ed25519 key
configured for Taiclock from being induced to sign a
message valid under another protocol that happens to
embed the same suffix. Operators SHOULD NOT reuse a
Taiclock signing key for any other purpose.

## DNS Trust

Verification reduces to trusting the DNS lookup of the
TXT record at `<selector>._taiclock.<host>`. A
verifier that does not validate DNS responses (for
instance via DNSSEC or a trusted resolver path) is
vulnerable to a DNS attacker who can substitute a
public key. Operators SHOULD publish under a DNSSEC-
signed zone where feasible.

## Clock Manipulation

A signature attests that the server emitted a given
label; it does not attest that the server's clock is
correct. An operator whose clock is drifting or
deliberately set wrong will sign whatever label its
clock produces. Verifiers that depend on Taiclock for
high-stakes decisions SHOULD compare readings from
multiple independently operated services.

## Leap-Second Reporting

The `TAI-Leap-Seconds` header is informational and is
not covered by the signature in any way that prevents
an operator from emitting a wrong value. The label
itself is the authenticated artefact; verifiers that
care about UTC equivalence MUST source the
TAI-to-UTC offset from a trusted table rather than
from the server.

